es as enforced at registration, grading weights, and required materials.',3,'ECO',1),('ECO149','Economics Analysis','Economics Analysis (ECO149) is a 3-credit course offered through Economics. Students engage with core concepts through readings, discussion, structured assessments, and (where applicable) laboratory or project work aligned to department learning outcomes. Consult the official syllabus each term for prerequisites as enforced at registration, grading weights, and required materials.',3,'ECO',1),('ECO158','Economics Systems','Economics Systems (ECO158) is a 3-credit course offered through Economics. Students engage with core concepts through readings, discussion, structured assessments, and (where applicable) laboratory or project work aligned to department learning outcomes. Consult the official syllabus each term for prerequisites as enforced at registration, grading weights, and required materials.',3,'ECO',1),('ECO201','Intermediate Microeconomic Theory','Formal models of choice, production, general equilibrium, and welfare economics. Problem sets emphasize calculus-based reasoning.',3,'ECO',1),('ECO210','Introduction to Econometrics','Probability review, OLS, inference, heteroskedasticity, and introduction to time series with statistical software labs using real datasets.',4,'ECO',1),('ECO301','International Economics','Trade theory, tariffs and quotas, exchange rates, balance of payments, and open-economy macro policy debates.',3,'ECO',1),('ENG101','English Composition I','English Composition I (ENG101) is a 4-credit course offered through Engineering. Students engage with core concepts through readings, discussion, structured assessments, and (where applicable) laboratory or project work aligned to department learning outcomes. Consult the official syllabus each term for prerequisites as enforced at registration, grading weights, and required materials.',4,'ENG',1),('ENG105','Engineering Topics','Engineering Topics (ENG105) is a 4-credit course of