ings, discussion, structured assessments, and (where applicable) laboratory or project work aligned to department learning outcomes. Consult the official syllabus each term for prerequisites as enforced at registration, grading weights, and required materials.',4,'ENG',1),('ENG150','Engineering Practice','Engineering Practice (ENG150) is a 4-credit course offered through Engineering. Students engage with core concepts through readings, discussion, structured assessments, and (where applicable) laboratory or project work aligned to department learning outcomes. Consult the official syllabus each term for prerequisites as enforced at registration, grading weights, and required materials.',4,'ENG',1),('ENG205','Engineering Thermodynamics','First and second laws, properties of substances, cycles, and efficiency analysis for thermal-fluid systems relevant to mechanical engineering practice.',4,'ENG',1),('ENG301','Fluid Mechanics','Statics and dynamics of fluids, Bernoulli and momentum equations, dimensional analysis, pipe flow, and introductory turbomachinery.',4,'ENG',1),('ENG310','Systems Dynamics and Control','Modeling mechanical and electrical systems, transfer functions, stability, PID concepts, and introductory feedback design with simulations.',3,'ENG',1),('ENG401','Senior Capstone Design','Year-long style semester project integrating analysis, design constraints, verification, documentation, and formal presentation to faculty and stakeholders.',4,'ENG',1),('ENGL101','English Composition I','Reading critically, drafting and revising essays, developing arguments, and integrating sources responsibly with emphasis on clarity and audience.',4,'ENGL',1),('ENGL102','English Composition II','Research-based writing, synthesis across sources, rhetoric, and revision strategies beyond first-year composition expectations.',4,'ENGL',1),('ENGL106','English Workshop','English Workshop (ENGL106) is a 5-credit course offered through English. Students engage with core concepts thro